"<span>Blind: hide the diffraction limit, the predictions and the wavefront error</span>",
"The picture is computed, not drawn. A star is so far away that its light arrives as a flat wavefront. The telescope should turn it into a sphere converging on one point; any error in that sphere is the wavefront error W, measured in wavelengths. The star's image is the diffraction pattern of the aperture with that error: the intensity is the squared size of the two dimensional Fourier transform of the aperture times e^({#}πiW/λ) (Fraunhofer diffraction), worked out with a fast Fourier transform on a {#} × {#} grid, the aperture {#} samples across at {#} nm.",
"W comes from tracing real rays. The mirror is the exact conic surface z = r²/(R[{#} + √({#} − ({#} + K) r²/R²)]), K = {#} for a sphere and −{#} for a paraboloid, with the stop at the mirror. Lenses are exact spherical surfaces with Snell's law, and the glass index n(λ) from the Schott Sellmeier formulas for N-BK7 and F2. The path of each ray is followed to a sphere centered on where the central ray meets the camera. The best fitting tilt is removed, so the picture stays centered on the star's own center of light. Where W changes by more than half a wave from one sample to the next, the grid cannot follow it, and the picture is made from the rays instead (geometric optics): the blur is then far bigger than diffraction anyway.",
"What to expect (third order theory, for comparison only: the picture uses the full trace). A spherical mirror's wavefront error at its edge is W = D/({#} N³), so halving the focal ratio makes it {#} times worse. Off the axis a parabola shows coma, a comet whose length is {#}θ/(16N²) for a star at angle θ, and, farther out, astigmatism. A single lens focuses blue (F line, {#} nm) and red (C line, {#} nm) a distance f/V apart, where V = (n_d − {#})/(n_F − n_C) is the glass's Abbe number: {#} for N-BK7 and {#} for F2. The Strehl ratio is the image's peak over a perfect telescope's peak; {#} or more counts as diffraction limited (Maréchal).",
"The air is a Kolmogorov phase screen: its structure function is {#} (r/r₀)^({#}/{#}), with r₀ given at {#} nm and growing as λ^({#}/{#}), so the same air blurs infrared light far less. Screens are made by the FFT method with three levels of subharmonics (Lane, Glindemann and Dainty {#}; Johansson and Gavel {#}), and the wind ({#} m/s) carries them across the aperture; the motion is shown slowed down {#} times. A long exposure averages {#} moments. The screens' largest scales are somewhat weaker than Kolmogorov's law, much as with a real outer scale of tens of meters, so the image moves a little less. Adaptive optics is modeled as perfect: it removes the first {#}, {#} or {#} Zernike shapes of the wavefront exactly and at once, with no delay or measurement noise.",
"Hubble: a {#} m Ritchey–Chrétien telescope at f/{#} (Allen report, {#}). Its primary (radius of curvature {#} m) was made with conic constant −{#} instead of the design's −{#}, about {#} µm too flat at the edge. Here the error is the difference of the two surfaces, doubled on reflection; it gives {#} waves rms at {#} nm after focusing, against the report's {#}. The secondary's shadow is {#} of the diameter and the four supports are {#} of the radius wide (the Tiny Tim pupil model). The corrective optics are modeled as cancelling the error exactly. The coma corrector is ideal: it removes the coma shape of the wavefront at every star position.",
"Approximations: scalar diffraction and the Fraunhofer limit; each filter is a single wavelength; white light is {#} equally weighted wavelengths from {#} to {#} nm, colored with the CIE {#} color matching functions (the fit of Wyman, Sloan and Shirley {#}) shown in sRGB; the brightest point of the picture is shown white; with Same brightness scale as a perfect telescope, the scale is fixed to the perfect telescope's peak instead, so a blurred star looks dimmer as well as bigger. A mirror telescope's camera is drawn at the prime focus, with no shadow of its own. All data are simulated.",
